    "created_at": "2026-10-13T17:05:21Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-13T17:06:56Z",
    "body": "IIUC, this would require adding a new primitive in addition to the existing [natural dimensions](https://drafts.csswg.org/css-images/#natural-dimensions), [density-corrected intrinsic dimensions](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/images.html#density-corrected-intrinsic-width-and-height), [concrete size](https://drafts.csswg.org/css-images/#concrete-object-size), and [default size](https://drafts.csswg.org/css-images/#default-object-size) -- \"actual asset dimensions\" or something -- and integrating it into the `object-fit` spec.\n\nI know the fact that actual asset dimensions are easy to reliably infer cross-origin is [considered a hole in the same-origin policy](https://css-tricks.com/i-learned-to-love-the-same-origin-policy/). Would adding this primitive and exposing it in how images are rendered (if not sized for layout) make that hole worse in any way?",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "pin": null,
